            <h2 id="sources-title" class="section-heading">Sources</h2>
            <ol>
                <li id="source-1"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11667504/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wang et al. (2024), semaglutide and first Alzheimer's diagnoses in people with diabetes</a>.</li>
                <li id="source-2"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41865758/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EVOKE and EVOKE+, <em>The Lancet</em> (2026), trials in early-stage Alzheimer's</a>.</li>
                <li id="source-3"><a href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dementia" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHO, dementia and risk factors</a>.</li>
                <li id="source-4"><a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2032183" target="_blank" rel="noopener">STEP1 (2021), weight loss in adults with overweight or obesity, without diabetes</a>.</li>
                <li id="source-5"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28110911/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SUSTAIN1 (2017)</a> and <a href="https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/9749/smpc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">detailed results in the official product information</a>. Target: HbA1c, a measure of average blood sugar, below 7%.</li>
                <li id="source-6"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9713290/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Verma et al. (2022), semaglutide and CRP, analyses of STEP1 to STEP3</a>.</li>
                <li id="source-7"><a href="https://www.ipsos.com/sites/default/files/ct/publication/documents/2025-11/Ipsos%20Health%20Service%20Report%202025%20final_0.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ipsos Health Service Report (2025), discussions of microdosing and longevity</a>. This describes a trend, not evidence of efficacy.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>
        <p class="disclaimer">General information, not medical advice. Discuss any treatment decision with your doctor.</p>
    </div>
</div>
